  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nWednesday...Dealing with the passing clipper, most models suggest it \nto be a rather vigorous one aided by some mid to upper jet support \nrounding it's base as it digs down right acrs the area. But several \nmodels including the available CAMs just skirt the north and then \neastern CWA with a sctrd lighter showers, and then out of the local \nCWA by late afternoon. The latest RRFS barely has anything or just a \nfew light showers and sprinkles skirting the northern CWA, probably \ntaking into account the dry airmass to overcome despite some good \nlift. All this in contrast to the aggressive and further south ECMWF \nwhich streaks light to moderate showers acrs most of the CWA, with \nrainfall amounts of 0.10 to 0.30 acrs the southern 3/4's of the DVN \nfcst area. This includes a few streaks of higher QPF of 0.40 to over \na half inch of rainfall along and south of I-80 by Wed evening. With \nthe uncertainty, will expand the 20-30 chance POPs acrs much of the \narea. With the vigorous wave and signs of mid level lapse rates \nreaching toward 7 C/km, there may even be a chance for an isolated \nthunderstorm especially if the ECMWF thermodynamic profiles pan out.\n\nTemps a challenge for Wednesday with cloud cover and precip/evapo-\ncooling processes and may lean toward the cooler side of guidance \nfor temps in the 50s. Post sfc wave LLVL pressure gradient and in-\nwrapping cold conveyor should make for blustery northwesterly sfc \nwinds Wed afternoon and evening of 15 to 25 MPH and higher gusts. \nCold advection to bring about Wed night lows well down in the 30s.\n\nThursday and Friday...Upper jet pattern and mass field evolution \non the latest medium range ensembles suggest a high amplitude \npattern acrs the CONUS this period, with a large deep upper trof \nacrs the GRT LKS and eastern CONUS, while a stout upper ridge edges \nup the Intermountain West and Rockies. A near meridional fetch \nbetween these two regimes down the Upper to Mid MS RVR Valley \nregions will allow colder Canadian air to spill down the western GRT \nLKS for a chilly end to the week for the local area. Highs in the \n40s and lows in the 20s through Saturday morning. A few models want \nto wrap some stratocu with embedded light precip down along and just \neast of the DVN CWA on Thursday with thicknesses low enough to bring \nabout precip type concerns, but those are the outliers for now and \nwill keep it dry.\n\nSaturday through Monday...Will keep it dry and cool for Saturday \ninto Sunday with a messy blocked pattern engulfing much of the \nCONUS. May have to watch for an under-cutting wave ejecting out of \nthe southwestern CWA and trying to whirl some precip up this way by \nSunday night, but again dry air in place and limited moisture feed \nmake this solution questionable. Longer range mass field signs of a \nstrong thermal ridge then trying to  build back from the west and \nacrs the upper Midwest could mean a return to a dry and well above \nnormal temp regime for the first half of next week.",
